1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,474
A system wins 90 trades out of 100.

2
00:00:08,924 --> 00:00:11,040
Sounds amazing.

3
00:00:11,489 --> 00:00:15,278
Yet at the end of the month, the account is negative.

4
00:00:15,728 --> 00:00:19,124
How? That is the maths we will see today.

5
00:00:20,124 --> 00:00:24,957
Hello, this is episode two of the SPIDER Quant Trading series.

6
00:00:25,406 --> 00:00:32,094
Last time we learned: an edge is a small advantage, and its number is expectancy.

7
00:00:32,543 --> 00:00:40,015
Today: what is win rate? What is payoff? And why can a system that wins often still lose?

8
00:00:41,015 --> 00:00:44,881
Win rate is simple: how many of 100 trades won.

9
00:00:45,331 --> 00:00:48,884
Won 70, so the win rate is 70%.

10
00:00:49,334 --> 00:00:55,368
But it only counts. It says nothing about how big the wins and losses were.

11
00:00:56,368 --> 00:00:59,372
That is where the second number comes in: payoff.

12
00:00:59,822 --> 00:01:05,072
Payoff means how big the average win is compared to the average loss.

13
00:01:05,522 --> 00:01:11,243
Average win 300, average loss 100. So the payoff is 3.

14
00:01:11,693 --> 00:01:16,448
Win rate tells you how often. Payoff tells you how much.

15
00:01:17,448 --> 00:01:20,033
Now, that 90% system.

16
00:01:20,484 --> 00:01:24,768
90 wins, 10 rupees each. Total 900.

17
00:01:25,217 --> 00:01:30,128
10 losses, but 100 rupees each. Total 1,000.

18
00:01:30,579 --> 00:01:34,705
900 minus 1,000. Net minus 100.

19
00:01:35,155 --> 00:01:41,425
It won 90 times and still lost, because the wins were small and the losses big.

20
00:01:42,425 --> 00:01:46,866
Now the opposite. A system that wins only 40%.

21
00:01:47,317 --> 00:01:51,601
But each win is 300 and each loss is 100.

22
00:01:52,050 --> 00:02:00,018
40 x 300 is 12,000. 60 x 100 is 6,000. Net plus 6,000.

23
00:02:00,467 --> 00:02:06,267
Won less, earned more. It is not about winning, it is about the maths.

24
00:02:07,267 --> 00:02:11,630
So how much win rate is enough? It depends on the payoff.

25
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:16,991
If wins and losses are equal, you need more than 50%.

26
00:02:17,441 --> 00:02:21,725
If wins are twice the losses, even 34% is enough.

27
00:02:22,174 --> 00:02:27,164
And if a win is one tenth of a loss, you need more than 91%.

28
00:02:27,614 --> 00:02:33,021
That is why our 90% system lost. It needed 91.

29
00:02:34,021 --> 00:02:36,216
There is an easy formula for this.

30
00:02:36,666 --> 00:02:42,150
Required win rate equals loss divided by win plus loss.

31
00:02:42,601 --> 00:02:49,028
Win 300, loss 100. 100 over 400, so 25%.

32
00:02:49,477 --> 00:02:54,727
Win more than that and the maths is positive. Less, and it is negative.

33
00:02:55,727 --> 00:03:00,718
Another truth: low win rate systems get losing streaks.

34
00:03:01,168 --> 00:03:06,156
In a 40% system, five losses in a row is normal.

35
00:03:06,607 --> 00:03:12,798
This is where people quit, right before the next big win.

36
00:03:13,248 --> 00:03:18,316
So before running a system, know how it loses too.

37
00:03:19,316 --> 00:03:23,286
High win rate systems have a hidden danger.

38
00:03:23,736 --> 00:03:29,065
Often they take small profits fast and let losses run long.

39
00:03:29,515 --> 00:03:34,426
For months everything looks green. Then one bad day wipes it out.

40
00:03:34,876 --> 00:03:39,866
So when you judge such a system, always look at its biggest loss.

41
00:03:40,866 --> 00:03:44,418
So in any system, look at three numbers together.

42
00:03:44,868 --> 00:03:51,216
One, win rate. Two, payoff. Three, the biggest loss.

43
00:03:51,666 --> 00:03:56,656
If someone sells a system showing only win rate, ask for the other two.

44
00:03:57,656 --> 00:03:59,222
So, today's summary.

45
00:03:59,673 --> 00:04:05,159
Win rate says how often you win. Payoff says how much.

46
00:04:05,608 --> 00:04:10,755
Required win rate equals loss over win plus loss.

47
00:04:11,204 --> 00:04:17,474
A 90% system can lose. A 40% system can earn.

48
